    "field": "Climate change modeling",
    "problem": "Most state-of-the-art climate models do not represent positive feedbacks from natural wetland methane emissions and permafrost thaw, leading to overestimated anthropogenic carbon emission budgets for meeting global warming targets and underestimating the required mitigation efforts.",
    "method": "The paper proposes using an inverted intermediate complexity climate model, which follows prescribed warming pathways and incorporates a land surface model with updated wetland and permafrost carbon release processes, to calculate the permissible anthropogenic CO2 emission budgets for 1.5\u00b0C and 2.0\u00b0C stabilization targets.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify how climate feedbacks from natural wetland methane and permafrost thaw reduce the permissible anthropogenic fossil fuel CO2 emission budgets required to stabilize global warming at 1.5\u00b0C and 2.0\u00b0C using an intermediate complexity climate model.",
